issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if (!this._serverToken || this._isJWTExpired(this._serverToken)) {
    const tokenData = this._generateToken({name, expiresInMinutes});
    this._serverToken = tokenData;
    this._serverTokenExp = tokenData.exp;
}

_isJWTExpired(token, bufferSeconds = 300) {
    try {
        const decoded = jwt.decode(token);
        if (typeof decoded !== 'object' || !decoded.exp) {
            return true;
        }
        const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
        const timeRemaining = decoded.exp - now;
        return timeRemaining < bufferSeconds;

if (!this._serverToken || this._isJWTExpired(this._serverToken)) {
    const tokenData = this._generateToken({name, expiresInMinutes});
    this._serverToken = tokenData;
    this._serverTokenExp = tokenData.exp;
}

findings.body:
`getToken()` now assigns the object returned by `_generateToken()` to `_serverToken`, not the JWT string. Two things go wrong:

1. The cache never works. `_isJWTExpired(this._serverToken)` receives an object, not a JWT, so it returns true. A fresh token is minted on every call.
2. The returned `token` is an object. `services/stats/utils/tinybird.js:82` builds `Authorization: Bearer ${token}`, so the header becomes `Bearer [object Object]` and every JWT-mode request is rejected. The `/tinybird/token` endpoint also returns a nested object as `token`.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="96-103"}

The fix is to store the string and keep the expiry separately.

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="97-100"}
const tokenData = this._generateToken({name, expiresInMinutes});
this._serverToken = tokenData.token;
this._serverTokenExp = tokenData.exp;

`_isJWTExpired` changes from `jwt.verify(token, adminToken)` to `jwt.decode(token)`. The old check rejected a token that had been tampered with or signed with a different key, so it would regenerate. The new check accepts any well-formed JWT whose `exp` is in the future. This is not in the PR description, and it is not needed for removing filters.

The current caller only passes tokens this service generated, so the practical risk is small. It is still a silent loss of a check, and it is the kind of change that breaks if the cache ever holds an externally supplied token. Keep `jwt.verify`, or add a comment explaining why decode is enough.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="161-170"}
